  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM MDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nSummer heat paired with daily rounds of afternoon and evening \nshowers and thunderstorms will remain the dominant weather \nnarrative through the weekend and into next week. A Heat \nAdvisory remains in effect today for lower-elevation zones. \nAmbient temperatures have already surged well into the 90s this \nafternoon, and a few isolated triple-digit readings remain \npossible across the lower Snake Plain and Magic Valley before \npeak heating subsides. With Saturday forecast to produce a near-\nidentical thermal profile, the Heat Advisory was pushed to cover\nSaturday as well. \n\nHigh-resolution convective-allowing models (CAMs) continue to \nshow isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms developing \nduring the afternoon and evening hours today and again on \nSaturday. This activity is driven by persistent southwesterly \nflow aloft, which continues to transport sub-tropical Pacific \nmoisture into the Intermountain West. Forecast soundings \nindicate that thermodynamic profiles will remain supportive of a\nmix of both wet and dry thunderstorms through the period as \nsouthwesterly flow lingers into mid-week. The National Blend of \nModels (NBM) continues to struggle with capturing the diurnal \ncycle of these terrain-driven convective pops in the medium \nrange. Consequently, expect baseline rain chances to be nudged \nupward as individual forecast days shift into the higher-\nresolution short-term window over the days ahead. \n\nLooking further ahead, the synoptic pattern remains heavily\nweighted toward above-normal heat across the region. The\npersistent Four Corners high-pressure ridge will remain firmly\nanchored through all of next week. This configuration will\nmaintain widespread lower-elevation highs in the mid-to-upper\n90s through the entire 7-day period, making additional heat-\nrelated headlines very likely as the pattern unfolds.",
